\title{Non-Well-Founded Proofs as Self-Similarity:\\ A Fixed-Point Theory of Safe Self-Reference}
\author{Harmonic}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a unified theory in which \emph{self-reference, when it is valid, is self-similarity}.
Classical logic treats self-referential statements with suspicion: the liar sentence and
G\"odel's diagonal lemma show that uncontrolled self-reference breaks completeness and consistency.
We propose a sharp dividing criterion---\emph{contraction}---that separates self-referential
definitions which name a unique object from those which name nothing. A self-referential equation
$x = f(x)$ has a unique solution exactly when $f$ shrinks distances by a constant factor below one;
the same condition governs which self-similar geometric objects are well-defined and which recursive
(proof) definitions converge. We realize the thesis through two engines---contraction fixed points
and coinductive data---and unify five phenomena under a single principle, \emph{a quantity that is
the unique solution of its own equation}: the geometric series, the coinductive geometric stream,
the affine attractor with explicit convergence rate, the metallic ratios with their gnomon
self-similarity, and the similarity dimension. We then transport the criterion to proof theory:
the self-referential proof of $P \Rightarrow P$ is a valid non-well-founded proof of finite height,
while the liar sentence is invalid precisely because its height functional $h \mapsto h+1$ is
non-contractive and admits no fixed point. The paradox becomes a boundary case rather than a flaw.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem of self-reference}
Self-reference is the engine behind the deepest negative results of twentieth-century logic.
The liar sentence has no stable truth value; G\"odel's first incompleteness theorem constructs a
sentence asserting its own unprovability; Russell's paradox exhibits a set that contains itself iff
it does not. The orthodox response is \emph{well-foundedness}: every legitimate definition and proof
must bottom out in a finite descending chain, forbidding circular dependency.

Yet mathematics is replete with self-referential objects that are entirely well behaved. The golden
ratio is defined by $\varphi = 1 + 1/\varphi$. A geometric series equals its first term plus a scaled
copy of itself. A fractal is, by construction, made of smaller copies of itself. None is paradoxical.
The aim of this paper is to explain \emph{why}, with a single criterion, and to use that criterion to
rehabilitate a controlled class of self-referential proofs.

\subsection{Thesis}
\begin{quote}
A self-referential definition $x = f(x)$ is valid---names exactly one object---if and only if its
defining operator $f$ is a contraction. Valid self-reference is self-similarity, and the unique named
object is the contraction's fixed point.
\end{quote}
The ``if'' direction is the Banach fixed-point principle; the ``only if'' direction is exhibited by the
family of non-contractive failures (the $r=1$ geometric series, the liar sentence) where existence or
uniqueness collapses.

\subsection{Two formal engines}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Contraction fixed points.} A map with Lipschitz constant $L<1$ has a unique fixed point
reached by iteration at geometric rate. This handles numbers, points, and finite-dimensional
self-reference.
\item \textbf{Coinduction.} Infinite objects are characterized by their observations (head/tail);
bisimulation supplies a uniqueness principle for self-referential infinite structures.
\end{itemize}

\section{Preliminaries and definitions}
Throughout, $\R$ carries its usual metric, and $f^{(k)}$ denotes the $k$-fold composition of $f$, with
$f^{(0)} = \mathrm{id}$.

\begin{definition}[Contraction]
A map $f:\R\to\R$ is a \emph{contraction with factor $L$} if $0 \le L < 1$ and
$|f(x)-f(y)| \le L\,|x-y|$ for all $x,y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Self-referential value / fixed point]
A point $x^\star$ is a \emph{fixed point} of $f$ if $x^\star = f(x^\star)$. We read the equation
$x = f(x)$ as a \emph{self-referential definition} of $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Geometric value]
For $a,r\in\R$ with $r\ne 1$, set $\geomVal(a,r) := \dfrac{a}{1-r}$. For $|r|<1$ this is the sum of the
convergent series $\sum_{n\ge0} a r^n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Geometric stream]
The \emph{geometric stream} $G_{a,r}$ has entries $G_{a,r}(n)=a r^n$. It is generated coinductively by
$\head(G_{a,r}) = a$ and $\taill(G_{a,r}) = G_{ar,r}$. We write $\scalee_r(s)$ for the stream obtained
by multiplying every entry of $s$ by $r$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Affine map]
For $c,b\in\R$, $A_{c,b}(x) = c x + b$; it is a contraction with factor $|c|$ when $|c|<1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Metallic ratio]
For an integer $m\ge1$, the \emph{$m$-th metallic ratio} is
$\varphi_m := \dfrac{m + \sqrt{m^2+4}}{2}$.
($\varphi_1$ golden, $\varphi_2 = 1+\sqrt2$ silver, $\varphi_3$ bronze.)
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Similarity dimension]
For $k\ge1$ and $0<r<1$, $\simDim(k,r) := \dfrac{\log k}{\log(1/r)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bisimulation of streams]
Streams $s,t$ are \emph{bisimilar} if there is a relation $\mathcal{R}$ with $s\mathrel{\mathcal{R}}t$
such that whenever $u\mathrel{\mathcal{R}}v$, $\head(u)=\head(v)$ and $\taill(u)\mathrel{\mathcal{R}}\taill(v)$.
The coinduction principle states that bisimilar streams are equal.
\end{definition}

\section{Main results}

\subsection{The geometric series is a self-referential value}

\begin{theorem}[Self-consistency]\label{thm:geom-sc}
For $r\ne1$, $S=\geomVal(a,r)$ satisfies $S = a + rS$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$a + r\cdot\frac{a}{1-r} = \frac{a(1-r)+ra}{1-r} = \frac{a}{1-r} = S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness]\label{thm:geom-uniq}
If $r\ne1$ and $x = a + rx$, then $x = \geomVal(a,r) = \dfrac{a}{1-r}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$x(1-r)=a$; divide by $1-r\ne0$. Uniqueness needs only $r\ne1$; interpretation as a convergent sum
needs $|r|<1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The boundary case is the paradox in miniature]
At $r=1$, $x = a+x$ forces $a=0$; it is then satisfied by every $x$ (ambiguity) and otherwise by none
(contradiction). Loss of contraction destroys uniqueness, exactly as the liar sentence destroys a
stable truth value (\S\ref{sec:proof}).
\end{remark}

\subsection{The geometric stream is self-similar and unique}

\begin{theorem}[Self-similarity law]\label{thm:stream-law}
$\scalee_r(G_{a,r}) = \taill(G_{a,r})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Entry $n$: $\scalee_r(G_{a,r})(n) = r\cdot a r^n = a r^{n+1} = G_{a,r}(n+1) = \taill(G_{a,r})(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bisimulation rigidity]\label{thm:stream-uniq}
If $\head(s)=a$ and $\scalee_r(s)=\taill(s)$, then $s = G_{a,r}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The hypothesis says $s$ obeys exactly the coinductive generator of $G_{a,r}$. Take
$\mathcal{R} = \{(s',G_{a',r}) : \head(s')=a',\ \taill(s')=\scalee_r(s')\}$; heads agree and tails stay
related (with head $a'r$). By coinduction, $s = G_{a,r}$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The affine attractor}

\begin{theorem}[Existence]\label{thm:aff-ex}
If $|c|<1$, then $x^\star = \dfrac{b}{1-c}$ satisfies $A_{c,b}(x^\star)=x^\star$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$c\cdot\frac{b}{1-c} + b = \frac{cb + b(1-c)}{1-c} = \frac{b}{1-c}$. (Theorem~\ref{thm:geom-sc} with
$a=b,r=c$.)
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness]\label{thm:aff-uniq}
If $|c|<1$ and $A_{c,b}(x)=x$, then $x = \dfrac{b}{1-c}$. (Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:geom-uniq}.)
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric error contraction]\label{thm:aff-err}
For all $x_0$ and $k\ge0$, $\bigl|A_{c,b}^{(k)}(x_0)-x^\star\bigr| \le |c|^k\,\bigl|x_0-x^\star\bigr|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $k$. Since $x^\star$ is fixed,
$A^{(k+1)}_{c,b}(x_0)-x^\star = c\,(A^{(k)}_{c,b}(x_0)-x^\star)$; take absolute values and apply the
inductive bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Convergence]\label{thm:aff-conv}
If $|c|<1$, then $A_{c,b}^{(k)}(x_0)\to x^\star$ for every $x_0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The error is $\le |c|^k|x_0-x^\star|\to0$; squeeze.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Metallic ratios}

\begin{theorem}[Defining quadratic]\label{thm:met-sq}
For every integer $m\ge1$, $\varphi_m^2 = m\varphi_m + 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\varphi_m^2 - m\varphi_m = \varphi_m(\varphi_m-m)
= \frac{m+\sqrt{m^2+4}}{2}\cdot\frac{-m+\sqrt{m^2+4}}{2} = \frac{(m^2+4)-m^2}{4} = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Continued-fraction form]\label{thm:met-cf}
For $m\ge1$, $\varphi_m>0$ and $\varphi_m = m + \dfrac{1}{\varphi_m}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Divide Theorem~\ref{thm:met-sq} by $\varphi_m>0$. Thus $\varphi_m$ is the fixed point of
$g(x)=m+1/x$, the value of $[m;m,m,\dots]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Golden specialization]\label{thm:met-gold}
$\varphi_1 = \dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Gnomon self-similarity]\label{thm:met-gnomon}
A rectangle of side ratio $\varphi_m:1$, after removing $m$ unit squares from the long side, leaves a
rectangle of the same ratio $\varphi_m:1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Removing $m$ unit squares leaves a $1\times(\varphi_m-m)$ rectangle of long-to-short ratio
$1/(\varphi_m-m)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:met-cf}, $\varphi_m-m = 1/\varphi_m$, so the ratio is
$\varphi_m$. The figure reproduces at scale $1/\varphi_m$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Similarity dimension}

\begin{theorem}[Balance equation]\label{thm:dim-spec}
For $k\ge1$ and $0<r<1$, $D=\simDim(k,r)$ satisfies $k\,r^{D}=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$r^D = \exp\!\big(\tfrac{\log k}{\log(1/r)}\log r\big)
= \exp\!\big(-\log k\big) = 1/k$, so $kr^D=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity]\label{thm:dim-pos}
For $k\ge2$ and $0<r<1$, $\simDim(k,r)>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\log k>0$ and $\log(1/r)>0$; the quotient is positive.
\end{proof}

\section{The unifying principle}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Phenomenon & Self-referential equation & Unique solution & Engine \\
\midrule
Geometric series & $S = a + rS$ & $a/(1-r)$ & contraction \\
Geometric stream & $\taill(s)=\scalee_r(s),\ \head=a$ & $G_{a,r}$ & coinduction \\
Affine attractor & $x = cx+b$ & $b/(1-c)$ & contraction \\
Metallic ratio & $x = m + 1/x$ & $\varphi_m$ & contraction \\
Similarity dimension & $k\,r^{D}=1$ & $\log k/\log(1/r)$ & contraction \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

In every row the object is defined by referencing itself, and the reference is \emph{guarded}---the
loop strictly shrinks. Guardedness yields existence \emph{and} uniqueness: the geometric content of
``valid self-reference is self-similarity.''

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A --- Banach iteration for self-referential values.}
Given $|c|<1$, offset $b$, start $x_0$, tolerance $\varepsilon$: iterate $x_{k+1}=cx_k+b$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:aff-err}, $k\ge \log(\varepsilon/|x_0-x^\star|)/\log|c|$ certifies the tolerance.
Complexity $O(\log(1/\varepsilon)/\log(1/|c|))$.

\paragraph{Algorithm B --- Continued-fraction evaluation of metallic ratios.}
Given $m\ge1$ and depth $N$: fold $g(x)=m+1/x$ from a seed. Since $|g'(\varphi_m)|=1/\varphi_m^2<1$,
the truncations converge geometrically (factor $\approx\varphi_m^{-2}$ per step) to $\varphi_m$.

\section{Transport to proof theory: non-well-founded proofs}\label{sec:proof}

Model a (possibly non-well-founded) proof as a structure that may contain a sub-derivation referencing
its own conclusion, and assign it an \emph{ordinal height}: the least ordinal bounding its dependency
chains, defined as the fixed point of the induced height functional.

\begin{proposition}[Validity criterion]\label{prop:crit}
A self-referential proof is valid iff its height functional admits a least fixed point---equivalently,
each unfolding of the self-reference occurs at a strictly smaller ordinal (guardedness).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[$P\Rightarrow P$ is valid of height $1$]
To prove $P\Rightarrow P$: assume $P$; discharge the subgoal $P$ immediately by the assumption. The loop
closes in one step; its height functional $h\mapsto1$ is constant, hence contractive, with fixed point
$h=1$. This mirrors Theorem~\ref{thm:geom-sc} in the degenerate guarded case, with uniqueness by the
coinductive argument of Theorem~\ref{thm:stream-uniq}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[The liar is invalid]
Let $L$ assert its own unprovability. A derivation of $L$ would reference a derivation of $L$ of the same
size; its height functional is $h\mapsto h+1$. The equation $h=h+1$ has no ordinal solution, so no fixed
point---hence no well-defined height---exists. This mirrors the $r=1$ geometric series (and $A_{c,b}$ with
$c=1$): loss of the contraction factor destroys the fixed point.
\end{proposition}

\noindent Thus self-referential proofs are stratified by Proposition~\ref{prop:crit}: guarded ones are
valid, while the liar, Russell's predicate, and the naive G\"odel diagonal are exactly the
non-contractive failures. The paradoxes are the precise complement of the convergent loops.

\section{Applications and discussion}

The contraction/guardedness criterion underlies fixed-point numerics (Newton, value iteration, Picard),
iterated-function-system fractals and fractal compression, self-similar design via metallic ratios, and
the productivity of corecursive definitions in programming-language semantics. The two engines agree on
the geometric series / geometric stream pair (Theorems~\ref{thm:geom-uniq} and \ref{thm:stream-uniq}),
technical evidence that ``self-reference $=$ self-similarity'' is one phenomenon, not an analogy. A full
calculus of non-well-founded proofs (normalization, cut-elimination) is left open; the geometric core is
complete and quantitative.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Closed.} Bisimulation rigidity; the metallic-ratio family (quadratic, continued fraction,
gnomon, golden specialization); the similarity-dimension balance and positivity.
\item \textbf{D1.} IFS attractor in $\R^n$: for $f(x)=Ax+b$ with $\|A\|<1$, unique $x^\star=f(x^\star)$,
$f^{(k)}(x_0)\to x^\star$, and $\|f^{(k)}(x_0)-x^\star\|\le\|A\|^k\|x_0-x^\star\|$.
\item \textbf{D2.} Coinductive geometric trees: depth-$d$ level holds $2^d$ copies of scale $r^d$;
total-measure recursion $M = 1 + 2rM$ gives $M = 1/(1-2r)$ for $2r<1$.
\item \textbf{D3.} Monotonicity of $\simDim(k,r)$ in $k$ and in $r$, with uniqueness of the exponent.
\item \textbf{D4.} Mixed-ratio IFS: similarity dimension as the unique $D$ solving the Moran equation
$\sum_{i=1}^k r_i^D = 1$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
A self-referential definition names a unique object exactly when its loop contracts, and the named object
is then a self-similar fixed point. This single criterion organizes the geometric series, infinite
self-similar streams, affine attractors, the metallic ratios, and fractal dimension, and draws a precise
line through proof theory---validating the self-referential proof of $P\Rightarrow P$ while diagnosing the
liar sentence as the canonical non-contractive loop. The paradox is not a wall but a boundary.

